summary:
v5 is less a Luna discovery harvest than a checked relay of upstream data. Of its 96,140 anchored activations, 86,031 (89.5%) reuse a branch that a candidate had already nominated: HFT supplied 61,508 (64%), the channel reviews 15,523 (16%) and v12 about 9%. Luna added about 10.5%, and 4.5k of those are branch picks for word-analysis topics (s02). Where HFT exists, a script-only union of HFT traces, channel subchannels and v12 anchors already holds a median 98.2% of the branches v5 activated per ayah (902 ayat; s09). The step that pays is therefore a deterministic digest built from v5's JSON wherever v5 ran, united with v5's upstream everywhere else, and enriched with the script layers v5 lacked:
- concordance and co-occurrence counts;
- definitional cross-references;
- cross-word branch pairs;
- early-source phrases;
- Turkish gloss losses;
- the full list of attested branches;
- branch_kind flags.
Opus then reads it in two stances: first a surah or pericope chain map that only assembles and sets nothing aside, then one ayah call that integrates and writes. What the writer leaves out goes into an unused index computed by script. There is no selector and no self-accounting.
The prototypes support this:
- The digest holds every watch-case ingredient. At 29:38: the eye-film branch, the dictionary definition leading to the spider of 29:41, and a script pair linking the eye film to the kohl branch of the ayah's own ص د د (rank 25 of 3,126 pairs). At 18:86: ḥamaʾ's 3-of-3 co-occurrence with ṣalṣāl (15:26/28/33). At 18:96: nafakha in 18 ayat, 10 with the horn and 6 with rūḥ. All 16 Fatiha ingredient branches are present.
- Verdict labels measurably filtered v5. Its global lane cited 'strong' rows at 17.8% and 'no value' rows at 0.3%, and every connection it cited for 18:86 was strong or medium. So the digest carries no labels, no epistemic status and no reject reasons. It also drops the containment and boundary fields, which are 76–78% negatively framed and are where v5's disclaimers came from.
- Across all 909 v5 ayat the digest's median is about 18.5k tokens (p90 about 28k). That is the size band in which the dictionary arm kept its full thinking.
Estimated cost: about $0.8–1.1 per ayah on claude -p tests and about $0.35–0.55 per ayah through the Batch API.

answers:
Main answer: Q3, yes with conditions. A step on top of v5 works if it reads v5's discovery JSON through a label-free digest and unites it with the upstream data v5 relayed, so that v5's rejects and narrows cost nothing. It must add the layers v5 structurally lacked:
- concordance counts (18:86 and 18:96 are absent from v5 without them);
- definitional and pair partners across words and ayat;
- early-source phrases;
- Turkish gloss losses;
- the full list of attested branches;
- branch_kind flags.
Opus synthesises in two stances: a chain map first, then one ayah call. Nothing selects before the writer, and an unused index is computed by script.

Extending to the 85% v5 never ran:
- Do not rerun v5's Luna lanes. They are about 90% relay, a script union reproduces 98% of their branches, their accept/reject step was a pruning layer that echoed labels, and a rerun would cost about $3k (v8 COSTS: ~$0.56/ayah).
- Tier 2 (2,214 ayat with HFT): the same digest built from HFT, channels and v12 by script. Tested on 29:38, 29:41, 29:45 and 100:1.
- Tier 3 (3,113 ayat in 24 long surahs without HFT): channels plus v12 (3,097 of 3,136 ayat have v12 findings, about 1.4 per ayah, which is thin), plus an optional cheap Luna-HFT run on the HFT packets that already exist, after a small validation.

Partly answers:
- Q1: the unharvested value is in not losing readings downstream and in script layers, not in more discovery.
- Q2a: memory is allowed, marked and checked by script. Data is pushed only for rare branches, counts, phrases and partners.
- Q5: quran-slm similarity is useful as a within-ayah pair ranker, used for ordering only.
